Yes. For a kubeadm Kubernetes cluster, when you downgrade, you need to consider different components on the control-plane (master) and worker.

Assume:

Current:  v1.34
Target:   v1.33
1. Control-plane / Master components

These are the main components you need to consider:

Component	Downgrade?	Notes
kubeadm	✅ Yes	Cluster/node lifecycle tool
kubelet	✅ Yes	Node agent
kubectl	Optional	Client tool; can use compatible version
kube-apiserver	⚠️ Yes	Control-plane component
kube-controller-manager	⚠️ Yes	Control-plane component
kube-scheduler	⚠️ Yes	Control-plane component
etcd	⚠️ Depends	Do not blindly downgrade; restore if required
CoreDNS	⚠️ Depends	Kubernetes addon
kube-proxy	⚠️ Depends	Usually update/downgrade according to cluster version

With kubeadm, the control-plane components such as:

kube-apiserver
kube-controller-manager
kube-scheduler

are normally managed as static pods.

You don't normally install them using dnf individually.

2. Worker node components

Worker nodes are simpler:

Component	Downgrade?
kubeadm	✅ Yes
kubelet	✅ Yes
kubectl	Optional
containerd	Usually ❌
runc	Usually ❌
CNI plugin	⚠️ Depends
kube-proxy	⚠️ Depends

The important ones are:

kubeadm
kubelet

The container runtime:

containerd

normally doesn't need to be downgraded just because Kubernetes is being downgraded.

3. Very important distinction

Think about the components in layers:

                Kubernetes
                    |
        ┌───────────┴───────────┐
        ↓                       ↓
   Control Plane              Worker
        |                       |
 kube-apiserver             kubelet
 kube-scheduler             kubeadm
 kube-controller            containerd
 etcd                       CNI
 kubeadm                    kube-proxy
Control plane
kubeadm
   ↓
kube-apiserver
kube-controller-manager
kube-scheduler
Worker
kubeadm
   ↓
kubelet
   ↓
containerd
   ↓
runc
4. What about etcd?

This is the most important part.

Don't think:

"I upgraded Kubernetes, therefore I just downgrade etcd."

No.

Before a production upgrade:

ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /backup/etcd.db

Then if the control plane/etcd state needs recovery, you can restore the appropriate snapshot.

Kubernetes upgrade
       |
       ↓
   etcd backup
       |
       ↓
   Upgrade
       |
    Failure
       |
       ↓
Restore/recover

etcd contains your cluster state, so treat it separately from kubelet and kubeadm.

5. For your interview, remember this
Worker downgrade
1. cordon
2. drain
3. kubeadm downgrade
4. kubelet downgrade
5. restart kubelet
6. verify
7. uncordon
Control-plane downgrade
1. Backup etcd
2. Backup/snapshot control-plane VM
3. kubeadm downgrade
4. Restore/recover control-plane components as appropriate
5. Check etcd
6. Check API server
7. Check scheduler/controller-manager
8. Verify cluster
One correction to keep in mind

A Kubernetes version downgrade is not generally a supported mirror image of an upgrade. The exact downgrade path depends on the Kubernetes versions and what kubeadm upgrade has already changed. For production, restoring/replacing from a known-good pre-upgrade state is usually safer than attempting an in-place control-plane downgrade.

If you're practicing this on your AlmaLinux kubeadm cluster, I can give you the exact v1.34 → v1.33 commands for master + worker, including the dnf commands and kubeadm steps.

is there any advance way to reinstall evrything again

Yes. In fact, for a worker node, rebuilding/reinstalling the node is often cleaner than trying to downgrade every Kubernetes component.

For a kubeadm cluster, there are two approaches:

Option 1 — In-place downgrade
v1.34
 ↓
downgrade kubeadm
 ↓
downgrade kubelet
 ↓
fix/reconfigure components

This can become messy, especially on the control plane.

Option 2 — Rebuild the node ⭐
Old node
   ↓
Drain
   ↓
Remove from cluster
   ↓
Clean Kubernetes
   ↓
Install target version
   ↓
kubeadm join
   ↓
Uncordon

For workers, I strongly prefer this approach when you're doing a lab or when the node is disposable.

Worker node rebuild — step by step

Suppose:

Control Plane  v1.34
Worker1        v1.34
Worker2        v1.33

You want Worker1 back to v1.33.

1. Drain Worker1

From the control plane:

kubectl drain worker1 --ignore-daemonsets
2. Remove Worker1 from the cluster
kubectl delete node worker1
3. On Worker1, reset kubeadm
kubeadm reset -f

Then remove Kubernetes configuration:

rm -rf /etc/kubernetes
rm -rf /var/lib/kubelet
rm -rf ~/.kube

Depending on your CNI, also clean its configuration:

rm -rf /etc/cni/net.d
4. Remove old Kubernetes packages

On AlmaLinux:

dnf remove -y kubeadm kubelet kubectl

You normally do not need to remove containerd.

5. Install the required version

For example, v1.33:

dnf install -y kubeadm-1.33.x kubelet-1.33.x

Check:

kubeadm version
kubelet --version
6. Get a new join command

On the control plane:

kubeadm token create --print-join-command

You'll get something similar to:

kubeadm join 10.0.0.4:6443 \
  --token xxxxx.xxxxx \
  --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:xxxxx
7. Join the worker again

Run that command on Worker1:

kubeadm join 10.0.0.4:6443 \
  --token xxxxx.xxxxx \
  --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:xxxxx

Then:

systemctl enable --now kubelet
8. Check from control plane
kubectl get nodes

You should see:

NAME      STATUS   ROLES           VERSION
master    Ready    control-plane   v1.34
worker1   Ready    <none>          v1.33
worker2   Ready    <none>          v1.33

But there's a version-skew issue here: you should not leave a worker below the control-plane version indefinitely. The supported version relationship matters.

What about rebuilding the master?

That's different.

You cannot simply do:

kubeadm reset -f

on your only control plane and expect the cluster to come back.

Your control plane contains:

etcd
API Server
Controller Manager
Scheduler
Cluster certificates/configuration

So for a single-master lab, the easiest "reinstall everything" approach is often:

Backup anything important
        ↓
Destroy master VM
        ↓
Create fresh VM
        ↓
Install target Kubernetes version
        ↓
kubeadm init
        ↓
Install CNI
        ↓
Join workers

That's essentially rebuilding the entire cluster.

For your lab

Since you're using a kubeadm AlmaLinux lab, if your objective is simply to practice:

"Upgrade v1.33 → v1.34 → rollback to v1.33"

I'd actually recommend practicing two scenarios:

Worker: drain → reset → reinstall → join

Entire lab cluster: destroy VMs → recreate → install desired version

It's much easier to understand than trying to manually downgrade every control-plane component.

