Exactly. You are correct. In production, you cannot delete/reinstall the control-plane node and expect Kubernetes to magically know the previous state.

The key is etcd.

Think of it this way

The control-plane VM contains the Kubernetes components:

Control Plane VM
├── kube-apiserver
├── kube-scheduler
├── kube-controller-manager
├── kubeadm
├── kubelet
└── etcd  ← cluster state

The actual Kubernetes cluster state is primarily stored in etcd:

etcd
├── Deployments
├── Services
├── ConfigMaps
├── Secrets
├── RBAC
├── Nodes
├── Namespaces
└── other Kubernetes objects

So if you destroy the control-plane VM without preserving/restoring etcd, you lose that state.

How production recovery works

Suppose you have:

Production

Master
v1.34
  |
  └── etcd
       |
       └── cluster state

Before upgrade:

1. Take etcd snapshot
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /backup/etcd-snapshot.db

Verify it:

ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /backup/etcd-snapshot.db

And ideally copy that snapshot outside the master VM.

For example:

S3 / backup server / external storage
2. Take VM/image backup

You also take a snapshot/backup of the control-plane VM.

Now you have:

             BEFORE UPGRADE
                   |
          ┌────────┴────────┐
          ↓                 ↓
     VM Snapshot        etcd Snapshot
If upgrade fails

You have two possible recovery approaches.

Approach A — Restore the VM

If the VM snapshot represents the known-good state:

Broken Master
     ↓
Restore previous VM snapshot
     ↓
Master comes back
     ↓
etcd comes back
     ↓
Cluster state preserved

This is usually the simplest recovery for a VM-based lab/production environment.

Approach B — Rebuild the control plane + restore etcd

This is the more involved disaster-recovery scenario.

Old Master
    X
    |
    ↓
Create new Master
    |
    ↓
Install same/appropriate Kubernetes version
    |
    ↓
Restore etcd snapshot
    |
    ↓
Start control-plane components
    |
    ↓
Cluster state returns

The important point is:

You don't recreate the cluster from scratch. You recreate the control-plane infrastructure and restore the cluster's state from etcd.

And there's another important point

For a multi-control-plane production cluster, you normally don't need to destroy the whole control plane for a single-node upgrade.

For example:

                 Load Balancer
                      |
          ┌───────────┼───────────┐
          ↓           ↓           ↓
       Master1     Master2     Master3
       v1.33       v1.33       v1.33

You can upgrade them one at a time.

Master1 → upgrade → verify
                    ↓
Master2 → upgrade → verify
                    ↓
Master3 → upgrade → verify

The other control-plane nodes keep the cluster available.

This is the production strategy you should remember
                 BEFORE UPGRADE
                       |
             ┌─────────┴─────────┐
             ↓                   ↓
       etcd snapshot         VM backup
             |
             ↓
       Upgrade CP-1
             |
          Verify
             |
       Upgrade CP-2
             |
          Verify
             |
       Upgrade CP-3
             |
          Verify
             |
       Workers one-by-one

If something goes wrong:

Worker failure
    ↓
Replace/restore worker

If one control-plane node fails:

CP failure
    ↓
Other CP nodes continue
    ↓
Repair/replace failed CP

If the whole control plane/etcd is lost:

Disaster
   ↓
Rebuild control plane
   ↓
Restore etcd snapshot
   ↓
Recover cluster state

So your thinking is right: "delete master → reinstall → join" is not a production rollback strategy for an existing cluster. That's appropriate only when you're intentionally rebuilding a disposable cluster.
